
I will explain the design and implementation choices i made in this project.
with an emphasis on Object Oriented Programming principles, separation of concerns,
deterministic randomness, and gameplay driven decisions.

One of the biggest motivations i had while building the project because of its size
and hardships i wanted to make as intuitive and maintainable as possible in terms of 
extensions changes and debuggs so i wont be lost in my own code while finding and fixing
problems.
To make everything connects easily from scratch i looked 
in point of view of blocks, so everything
is alligned to blocks size, velocity of the avatar: both jumping and horizontal movement.
placement of trees and terrain,top trees,fruits leaves and more.
this way of thiking made everything easier and simetriclly, so building the world and the
game was much more intuitive, and easier.

The main goals of my implementation were:
- Clear responsibility boundaries between classes.
- Preserving encapsulation and minimizing coupling.
- Using the game engine correctly (layers, tags, collision normals, scheduled tasks).
- Interpreting the instructions in a way that improves gameplay clarity, stability,
  and overall playability, even when i found it required additional constraints or logic.

================================================
1. Avatar implementation
================================================

1.1 General design approach
---------------------------

I chose to implement the Avatar such that all movement logic, animation logic,
and energy logic are contained within the Avatar class itself, and are supported
by helper concepts such as enums, constants, and small helper methods.

The Avatar is the central gameplay entity that represents the player, and therefore
it is the natural place to concentrate all logic that decides:
- how the character moves,
- when actions are legal,
- how animations are selected,
- and how energy is consumed or regenerated.

This avoids scattering player behavior across multiple classes and keeps a single,
coherent authority over the avatar state.

------------------------------------------------
1.2 Classes, packages, and relationships
------------------------------------------------

pepse.world.avatar.Avatar

Avatar extends GameObject and is responsible for:
- Reading user input.
- Translating raw key presses into logical movement requests.
- Managing movement state (idle / running / jumping).
- Managing animation state.
- Managing the energy resource.
- Applying physics based movement : velocity updates.
- Handling collisions relevant to the avatar.

Avatar depends on:
- UserInputListener for input.
- AnimationRenderable / Renderable for visual representation.
- WorldTags for identifying collision targets by tag rather than by type.

Avatar deliberately does not depend on any display class.

pepse.display.AvatarEnergyPercentDisplay

This class is responsible only for displaying the avatar’s energy percentage.
It does not compute energy, does not decide when energy changes, and does not
contain gameplay logic.

Its only responsibility is to reflect a numeric value visually.

pepse.PepseGameManager

The game manager acts as a mediator:
- It creates the Avatar.
- It creates the energy display.
- It connects them using a callback mechanism.

This design ensures that the Avatar and the display do not know about each other,
preserving encapsulation and keeping coupling low.

------------------------------------------------
1.3 Avatar state changes and movement logic
------------------------------------------------

I explicitly modeled avatar behavior using logical states rather than relying only
on raw velocities.

The Avatar internally reasons about:
- Whether it is on the ground or in the air.
- Whether it is rising or falling : for the second jump only allowed while falling.
- What the current movement request is : left, right, none, jump.
- What the resulting movement state should be (idle / run / jump).

These decisions are expressed explicitly using enums such as:
- AvatarPosition (ON_THE_GROUND / IN_THE_AIR)
- AvatarAirSituation (RISING / FALLING)
- MovementRequest (high-level intent derived from input)
- MovementState (used for animation and energy logic)

This separation allows:
- Clear legality checks (for example, jump rules differ when rising vs falling).
- Precise control over energy costs per situation, jumping is one time update,
 but idling in the ground
- is per update similiar to running which also is per update.
- Cleaner animation switching logic.
- Easier debugging and reasoning about behavior.

The avatar figuers out its position either its on the ground/trunk in the help
of a set of gameobjects called floor objects that in collision from above 
in case it collieded trunks/terrain then it adds the objects to the set.
in the exit of coliision it removes the object from the set so in that way
the avatar knows either its on ground/trunk or in the air if the set is empty or not.
------------------------------------------------
1.4 Energy logic inside the Avatar
------------------------------------------------

Energy is an internal resource owned entirely by the Avatar.

Design principles:
- Only the Avatar can modify its energy.
- Energy changes only through well defined actions : jumping, running, idiling, eating a fruit.
- Energy is always clamped to a legal range from 0 to 100

This guarantees:
- A single source of truth for energy.
- No duplicated or conflicting logic in other classes.

------------------------------------------------
1.4.1 Additional threshold for starting a run , preventing flickering logic:
------------------------------------------------

During testing, especially with high FPS values, i encountered rapid oscillations
between very small energy values 0 -> 1 -> 2 -> 0 and then really quick to 2
again, so we get the flicker situation which looks bad and uninviting for a potential
player.

This caused visible flickering:
- The avatar would briefly start running,
- immediately stop,
- then start again,
leading to unstable movement and animation flicker.

To solve this, i introduced an additional rule:
- Starting a run requires at least 10 energy.
- Continuing a run only requires enough energy to pay the running cost.
In the way the range of 2 to 10 is like a mediator that fixed the problem.


This creates a distinction between:
- “Can I start running?”
- “Can I keep running?”

This design choice was made following a question that was asked in the course Q&A forum,
where it was clarified that introducing such a threshold is one of the valid solutions
and should be documented in the README.

Tradeoff:
- Slightly less responsiveness at very low energy levels.
Benefit:
- Much smoother movement and stable animations.

------------------------------------------------
1.5 Energy display design – Observer pattern
------------------------------------------------

The energy display was designed according to the Observer design pattern.

Roles:
- Subject: Avatar (owns the energy state).
- Observer: AvatarEnergyPercentDisplay (shows energy).
- Mediator: PepseGameManager (connects them).

How it works:
- Avatar exposes a callback setter for energy changes.
- PepseGameManager registers the display’s update method as the callback.
- When the Avatar’s energy changes, it notifies via the callback.

Important details:
- Avatar notifies only when its energy actually changes.
- The display updates only when the received value differs from the previous one.
- The display starts with an initial value of -1, which is outside the legal energy range,
  ensuring an immediate update at game start.

Why this design:
- Avatar and display do not know about each other directly.
- Encapsulation is preserved: no internal fields are exposed.
- Coupling is minimal and responsibilities are clearly separated.

------------------------------------------------
1.6 Jump height and running distance constants design using block units and physics:
------------------------------------------------

The avatar known world is in terms of blocks size.
so to make it intuitive i wanted to do it easily to change game constants,
so the avatar velocity x is defined in terms of size blocks, which means how
many block it can run in an horizontel move.
so i made the horzintel x velocity movement be depend on the asked number of block to move
multiplied in the size of a single block.

I did the same with jump, because:
height is defined in terms of the game worlds basic unit: blocks.
The desired jump height is specified as a number of blocks, and the initial jump
velocity is computed using a standard physics equation for motion under constant
acceleration:
v = sqrt(2 * g * h)
This equation was used with the help of online references.

Benefits:
- Both jump height and running distance can be adjusted by changing
 a single intuitive parameter.
- Jump behavior stays consistent with world scale.
- Tree heights can be balanced against avatar capabilities to create a challenging
  but playable experience.

------------------------------------------------
1.7 Correct landing detection using collision normals
------------------------------------------------

After adding tree trunks, i encountered an issue where the avatar could touch
a trunk from the side during a jump and be incorrectly considered “on the ground”.

To fix this, i used collision normals provided by the engine.
A collision is considered a landing only if the collision normal indicates
contact from above.

A threshold value of -0.8 for the Y component of the normal was selected
through trial and error and proved reliable in distinguishing real landings
from side friction.
------------------------------------------------
1.8 Animation design and state-driven animation logic
------------------------------------------------
The avatar animation system is designed to be driven by explicit logical states,
and kept consistent with the same concepts the Avatar already uses for movement
and energy decisions.

The animation is not chosen directly from a single raw value.
Instead, the Avatar:
- Reads input and translates it into MovementRequest values.
- Determines whether it is on the ground or in the air (AvatarPosition).
- Uses the resolved situation together with the movement request to compute
  a MovementState (IDLE / RUN / JUMP).
- Displays the animation that corresponds to the resolved MovementState.

The main enums that drive the behavior are:
- AvatarPosition (ON_THE_GROUND / IN_THE_AIR)
- AvatarAirSituation (RISING / FALLING)
- MovementRequest (high-level intent derived from input)
- MovementState (IDLE / RUN / JUMP)

------------------------------------------------
1.8.1 How MovementState maps to animations
------------------------------------------------

The Avatar maintains three AnimationRenderable objects:
- idleAnimation
- runAnimation
- jumpAnimation

Each frame, after movement and energy logic are resolved, the Avatar:
1. Updates the relevant AnimationRenderable (run/jump/idle) using update(deltaTime).
2. Calls updateAvatarAnimationByState(movementState), which:
   - Switches the renderer’s Renderable only when the MovementState changes.
   - Always applies horizontal flipping based on the current facing direction.

This ensures that animation switching is stable:
- If the state did not change, we do not replace the Renderable again.
- We still update facing direction every frame using setIsFlippedHorizontally().

------------------------------------------------
1.8.2 Using both states and physics signals (velocity) consistently
------------------------------------------------

While the design is state-based, the code also uses simple physics signals
(velocity and collision tracking) as part of determining the correct state.

Examples:
- Ground vs air detection uses a tracked set of floor objects (floorObjects),
  and also treats velocity.y == 0 as on-ground.
- Rising vs falling is determined using velocity.y.
- On the ground, “actually moving” is verified using velocity.x != 0.

This combination keeps the logic robust:
- Input expresses intention (MovementRequest).
- Velocity/collisions confirm the physical reality of motion and contact.

------------------------------------------------
1.8.3 Air behavior: RUN vs JUMP animations
------------------------------------------------

In this implementation, being in the air does not automatically imply the JUMP animation.

When AvatarPosition == IN_THE_AIR:
- If the player is requesting horizontal movement (MOVE_LEFT or MOVE_RIGHT),
  MovementState is set to RUN, and the run animation is shown (even in air).
- Otherwise, MovementState is set to JUMP, and the jump animation is shown.

This matches the gameplay choice that “airborne movement” still looks like motion,
while a neutral airborne state uses the dedicated jump sequence.

------------------------------------------------
1.8.4 Flicker prevention and smooth transitions
------------------------------------------------

The anti-flicker energy threshold for starting a run (Section 1.4.1)
also stabilizes the animation system.

Because starting to run requires MIN_ENERGY_TO_START_RUNNING,
the avatar avoids rapid oscillation between IDLE and RUN when energy is very low.
This directly reduces visual animation flicker and keeps movement feedback readable.

------------------------------------------------
1.8.5 Extensibility
------------------------------------------------

The animation logic is centralized in the MovementState  -> Renderable mapping.

To add future animations, the code structure supports:
- Adding a new MovementState enum value.
- Adding a matching AnimationRenderable.
- Extending updateAvatarAnimationByState with a new mapping case.

This keeps the logic maintainable and avoids scattering animation switching
across unrelated code paths.


================================================
2. Trees package implementation
================================================

2.1 Overview of responsibilities
--------------------------------

pepse.world.trees.Flora

Flora is the global planner of vegetation.
It decides:
- Where trees exist.
- Enforces spacing and placement constraints.
- Determines neighboring trees.
- Passes spatial constraints to individual Tree instances.

pepse.world.trees.Tree

Tree represents a single tree and is responsible for:
- Building the trunk.
- Planning the top tree grid.
- Creating leaves and fruits according to placement rules.

pepse.world.trees.Leaf

Leaf is responsible only for leaf behavior:
- Visual appearance.
- Wind-like animation.

pepse.world.trees.Fruit

Fruit is responsible for:
- Being collected by the avatar.
- Becoming temporarily unavailable when collected.
- Reappearing after a delay.

------------------------------------------------
2.1.1 Class relationships inside the trees package
------------------------------------------------

Dependencies are strictly one directional:

Flora ->  Tree ->  (Leaf, Fruit)

Flora performs world level planning and never manages runtime behavior.
Tree performs structure level planning for a single tree.
Leaf and Fruit encapsulate their own runtime behavior.

This avoids circular dependencies and prevents any class from becoming
the class that does everything

------------------------------------------------
2.1.2 Flora implementation goals – gameplay-driven constraints
------------------------------------------------

The Flora implementation was explicitly designed to support a playable,
readable, and challenging game experience.

Flora is random by design with constraints i addded for clarity and challanging
game, the way of design i did that works in a way that those constraints
can be easily removed or changed to get fully randomised flora without
constraints at all if needed.

While randomness is essential for producing a natural and varied world,
unconstrained randomness can easily produce edge cases that harm gameplay.

The constraints introduced in Flora serve the following purposes:
- Ensuring a playable spawn area.
- Preventing trees from clustering too tightly.
- Avoiding excessive concentration of fruits.
- Preserving visual clarity and navigation readability.

Randomness is not removed or weakened by these constraints.
Instead, constraints filter out rare random outcomes that would harm gameplay,
while preserving a wide and rich random design space.

------------------------------------------------
2.1.3 Segment-based deterministic randomness in Flora
------------------------------------------------

Flora divides the world into fixed planning segments (regions).
Each region:
- Has a fixed width measured in blocks.
- Is associated with a deterministic random generator derived from:
  (world seed, region index).

For each region:
- A probability check decides whether a tree exists in that region.
- If a tree is to be placed, a trunk column is selected randomly inside the region,
  subject to spacing and safety constraints.

This design ensures:
- Deterministic generation in terms of :  same seed -> same world.
- Stable streaming in way of : loading/unloading regions in any order gives the same result.
- Natural but controlled distribution of trees.

------------------------------------------------
2.1.4 Preventing a trunk at the avatar spawn position
------------------------------------------------

Because generation is fully deterministic, some seeds naturally lead to a trunk
being placed exactly at the avatar’s spawn column.

This would immediately block movement and break the game.

To prevent this:
- The avatar spawn X position is defined once in PepseGameConstants.
- Flora receives this value and converts it to a column index.
- Any candidate trunk column equal to the avatar spawn column is rejected.

This guarantees that every seed produces a playable starting state,
while keeping the rest of the world fully random.

------------------------------------------------
2.2 Dynamic top tree sizing and neighbor constraints
------------------------------------------------

Top tree sizes are restricted based on distances to neighboring trunks.

This prevents:
- Overlapping top trees.
- Excessive fruit clustering.
- Ambiguous collection behavior.

Top tree half widths are computed independently for the left and right sides,
which allows asymmetric but valid shapes, so the random doesnt hardly impected.

The main motivation was a gameplay clarity for example if a fruit is spawned
 in full randomized situation
inside a trunk then the avatar will not be able to collect the fruit.
another example is if two trees are really close and the top trees of both
 intersect so some fruits may
appear on each other, or really close to each other, so the avatar
 will be able to collect
some number of fruit in a single hit, which might be confusing to get energy
 from 10 for example to 80 because there are
7 fruits on top of each other or really close.
so i added the constarints so the game will be more challanging and clear, for the player.

------------------------------------------------
2.3 Top tree matrix design and placement rules:
------------------------------------------------
The matrix is built squered but is not squered by logic, because 
different size of the top tree might be in different sizes accoridng
to the constarints i made that will be explained.
The top tree is represented as a 2D matrix with enum states:
- EMPTY
- ILLEGAL
- LEAF
- FRUIT

Illegal cells include:
- Cells outside allowed horizontal bounds.
- Cells intersecting terrain.
- The trunk column : for fruit placement.

Placement order:
1. Mark ILLEGAL cells.
2. Place leaves in EMPTY cells.
3. Place fruits only in EMPTY cells that are structurally supported by leaves.

------------------------------------------------
2.3.2 Top tree matrix as a rule based structure:
------------------------------------------------

The top tree is modeled as a 2D matrix whose purpose is not only spatial
representation, but rule enforcement.

Rather than placing leaves and fruits directly in the world using conditions,
the matrix acts as an intermediate logical model that represents which placements
are legal, illegal, or already occupied.

Each cell in the matrix represents a potential position relative to the trunk,
and is labeled using an enum value (EMPTY, ILLEGAL, LEAF, FRUIT).

This design was chosen intentionally for several reasons:

First, the matrix provides a clear and centralized representation of the placement rules.
Instead of scattering conditional logic across multiple checks and random attempts,
all legality constraints are encoded explicitly in the matrix state.

This makes the logic:
- Easier to reason about.
- Easier to debug.
- Easier to validate against the assignment requirements.

Second, the matrix enforces a strict placement pipeline:
1. Determine which cells are structurally illegal (terrain intersection, trunk column,
   neighbor tree overlap, out-of-bounds).
2. Randomly place leaves only in EMPTY cells.
3. Place fruits only in cells that satisfy the “between leaves” rules.

At no point is a fruit placed arbitrarily.
Every fruit placement is validated against the matrix state, which guarantees
that fruits are always structurally connected to the top tree.

Third, the matrix design makes the “rule set” explicit and configurable.

Because legality is encoded by marking cells as ILLEGAL, the system can easily be tuned:
- If the requirement was full randomness, it would be enough to remove (or weaken)
  some of the ILLEGAL marking rules, and then the algorithm would naturally allow:
  - leaf overlap with terrain,
  - fruit clusters,
  - and top tree shapes that can intersect the ground or neighboring trees.

In other words, the matrix is not a hard coded top tree shape generator.
It is a rule engine: by changing the rules that produce ILLEGAL cells, we change
the entire top tree “legal world” without rewriting the placement algorithm itself.

This connects directly to the design philosophy described earlier in this README:
constraints are not meant to remove randomness, but to prevent rare random outcomes
that harm gameplay clarity and navigation readability.

The chosen constraints intentionally favor:
- A clearer and more defined top tree silhouette.
- Reduced visual noise from ground overlapping leaves.
- More readable fruit placement (collectibles are easier to locate and understand).
- Less ambiguous interactions between neighboring trees.

Fourth, this design follows the Open/Closed Principle.

The Tree class is:
- Closed for modification: the placement pipeline does not need to change.
- Open for extension: new top tree elements can be added by extending the enum and
  adding an additional placement phase that operates on the matrix.

For example, future top tree features could include:
- FLOWER
- NEST
- BIRD
- SNOW
- DECORATION

Each such feature could be added by:
- Extending the enum.
- Adding new rule marking and/or a new placement pass.

No existing placement logic would need to be rewritten.

Finally, the matrix cleanly separates responsibilities:
- Flora decides global spatial constraints and passes them to the Tree.
- Tree plans legality and structure using the matrix.
- Leaf and Fruit focus only on runtime behavior.

Tree never needs to ask:
“Is this position allowed?”
It already knows the answer from the matrix.

In summary, the top tree matrix is not an implementation detail,
but a deliberate architectural decision that encodes rules,
supports extensibility, and improves long-term maintainability.

------------------------------------------------
2.3.1 Fruit placement rule (“between leaves”)
------------------------------------------------

A fruit cell is legal if at least one of the following holds:
- A leaf exists directly below.
- Leaves exist on both left and right.
- A leaf exists above and on one side (left or right).

This ensures fruits are visually and structurally part of the top tree,
rather than floating arbitrarily.

The top tree matrix easily lets changing the rules or deciding where 
fruits might be spawned according to leaves
placement.

------------------------------------------------
2.4 Leaf design
------------------------------------------------

Leaf behavior (color and wind animation) is encapsulated entirely in the Leaf class.
Tree only decides placement, not behavior.

ADDITION – Leaf detailed implementation:
Each leaf is created using Leaf.create(topLeftCorner, seed).
The leaf-specific randomness is deterministic and derived from:
Objects.hash(seed, (int) topLeftCorner.x(), (int) topLeftCorner.y()).
This means:
- the exact same leaf cell always produces the same motion parameters,
  even if the world is unloaded and reloaded.
- different leaf cells do not share the same random sequence, preventing "synchronized" motion.

The Leaf class creates a GameObject of block size (Block.SIZE) and attaches:
1) A ScheduledTask with a random delay in [0, MAX_LEAF_MOVEMENT_DELAY].
   This prevents all leaves in a tree from starting to animate simultaneously.
2) Two back-and-forth transitions that simulate wind:
   - Rotation transition on renderer().setRenderableAngle
     with an angle sampled in [MIN_LEAF_WIND_MOVING_ANGLE, MAX_LEAF_WIND_MOVING_ANGLE].
   - Scaling transition using leaf::setDimensions between:
     leafMinSize (shrink) and leafMaxSize (grow),
     sampled using factors in [MIN_LEAF_SHRINK_FACTOR, 1] and [1, MAX_LEAF_GROW_FACTOR].

The important design idea is separation:
- Tree is responsible for structure and legality.
- Leaf is responsible for runtime animation and "alive" feeling.

This makes the system easy to extend:
If more leaf behaviors are needed (sound, particle, interaction),
they can be added inside Leaf without changing Tree or Flora.

------------------------------------------------
2.5 Fruit disappearance and reappearance
------------------------------------------------

When collected:
- The fruit becomes invisible.
- The fruit becomes non collidable.
- A scheduled task is created to respawn it.

When respawned:
- Visibility is restored.
- Collisions with the avatar are enabled again.

Fruit has its own layer and collision rules.
In the GameManager it was decided that the fruit layer and the avatar layer
suppossed to collide. which means because of the game engine rules that
only avatar and fruit supposed to collide because different layers are not
supposed to collide unless decided other wise.
so fruits collides only with the avatar, and only while available.
because of the override of the shouldcollide with method.
so the gamemanger decideds that those layers suppose to collide,
but each object independently decides what should happen in collission.
so in that way when fruit becomes non collidable it means cause of the layer 
logic from the game engine cause it was decided only avtar layer migt collide 
fruit layer that the fruit becomes non collidable to any object.
this decision was made by the gamemanager because he is the authority for that.
if tomorrow we want that for example there will be birds in the game and the birds
can eat fruits so we only need to make the birds layer and the fruit layer collidable 
and then birds can decide what happenss in that situation in terms of birds world. 
maybe more energy or maybe not flying no more.
and the fruit decides either it disappeares or not, since its a bird and birds eat in small amount
so maybe in case of birds collision the fruit doesnt need to disappeare.
so in summary the this disappearance of the fruit is easy to extend and well encapsulate in
terms of fruit agains gamemanager decisions which are more in the macro terms.

So in that way we get that :
Energy updates are handled by the Avatar, not by Fruit,
preserving encapsulation and avoiding circular dependencies.
which means avatar only decides when i collide fruit what should happen
