------------------------------------------------
ADDITION – Tree detailed implementation and randomness (height / top tree / leaves / fruits)
------------------------------------------------

The Tree class is responsible for building a single tree in a reproducible but varied way.

1) Trunk height randomness:
Trunk height is selected by:
generateRandomTrunkHeightInBlocks(randomGenerator)
with values in:
[MIN_TRUNK_HEIGHT_IN_BLOCKS, MAX_TRUNK_HEIGHT_IN_BLOCKS].

This randomGenerator is derived from:
new Random(makeFloraSeed(seed, trunkColumn))
inside Flora.
Therefore:
- Each trunk column always produces the same trunk height (deterministic).
- Different trunks get different heights because trunkColumn differs.

2) Trunk block construction:
The trunk is built as a vertical stack of Block objects:
- bottom aligned just above the terrain top at the trunk x.
- each block placed one Block.SIZE above the previous.

Trunk colors are sampled deterministically:
Random trunkRandom = new Random(Objects.hash(seed, trunkColumnWorldX))
and then approximateColor(BASE_TRUNK_COLOR, trunkRandom).
This produces a consistent "wood texture variation" per trunk.

3) Top tree size randomness:
The top tree grid size is controlled by the chosen half width:
generateRandomTopTreeHalfWidthInBlocks(randomGenerator, minHalfWidth, maxHalfWidth).

Then:
topTreeFullWidth = 2 * halfWidth + 1
and the grid is created as a square matrix of that size.
but the constarints on each side of the top tree matrix might cause 
the matrix become non squre in terms of legal places. which means
the matrix in theory is squared so it will be easy to maintain and change
but in reality it doesnt have to be squared because of the randomness and constraints
which gives more natural looks to top trees in the game.

4) Neighbor constraints integration:
Although the top tree size is randomized, the allowed half widths to left and right
are restricted based on neighbor trunks:
allowedTopTreeHalfWidthLeftInBlocks
allowedTopTreeHalfWidthRightInBlocks

This affects legality marking inside the grid:
cells outside the allowed range become ILLEGAL.
So the top tree remains random but "clipped" by neighbor constraints in a rule-based way.

5) Random leaf count and placement:
Leaf count is randomized by:
generateRandomLeafCount(randomGenerator, gridSize)
bounded by MIN_LEAF_COUNT and MAX_LEAF_COUNT (and capped by grid capacity).

Leaves are placed by random sampling of (row, col) cells:
- only EMPTY cells can become LEAF.
- attempts are limited by MAX_RANDOM_PLACEMENT_ATTEMPTS_MULTIPLIER to avoid infinite loops.

6) Random fruit count and placement:
Fruit count is randomized similarly, then fruits are placed only in:
- EMPTY cells
- cells that satisfy isLegalFruitCell (the “between leaves” rule)
- never in the trunk column (explicit check).

Each fruit’s visual randomness is deterministic:
ColorSupplier.approximateColor(BASE_FRUIT_COLOR, new
 Random(Objects.hash(seed, x, y, FRUIT_TAG))).

Summary:
Tree randomness is "two-level":
- Structure randomness (height, top tree width, leaf/fruit counts) 
comes from a deterministic Random
  derived from the world seed and trunk column.
- Local visual/animation randomness (leaf wind, fruit/trunk colors) 
is derived from position-based seeds.
This ensures variety without breaking reproducibility.

================================================
3. Additional classes beyond questions 1–2
================================================

Beyond the mandatory requirements of questions 1–2, i introduced several
additional infrastructure classes whose sole purpose is to improve coordination
between game systems, enforce consistency, and maintain encapsulation.

These classes do not add gameplay mechanics.
Instead, they act as shared reference points that allow different systems
(avatar, world, flora, terrain, day/night cycle, Display) to communicate indirectly
without creating tight coupling.

------------------------------------------------
3.1 WorldConstants – world-wide spatial coordination
------------------------------------------------

WorldConstants provides shared constants and utility methods related to world
geometry and coordinate calculations.

Its main responsibilities are:
- Defining how the world relates to the window size for example : base ground height.
- Providing canonical conversions between continuous world coordinates
  and the discrete block grid.
- Ensuring all classes use the same logic for block alignment.

Examples of responsibilities:
- Computing the base ground height at x=0 based on window height.
- Aligning X/Y coordinates to multiples of Block.SIZE.
- Flooring Y coordinates consistently when interacting with terrain.

This class is used by:
- Terrain (to align ground blocks).
- Flora and Tree (to detect ground overlap and plan tree geometry).
- PepseGameManager (to convert camera position to region indices).

Why this class exists:
Without WorldConstants, each system would have to re-implement its own
rounding and alignment logic, which would easily lead to subtle inconsistencies
(for example : leaves intersecting terrain, trees floating or sinking, mismatched regions).

By centralizing these calculations:
- All systems agree on what “aligned to the grid” means.
- Changes to world scale or block size affect the entire world consistently.
- Encapsulation is preserved, because no class needs to inspect another class’s internals.

------------------------------------------------
3.2 WorldTags – semantic identification without type checks
------------------------------------------------

WorldTags centralizes all string tags used to identify game objects.
different tags of different game objects.
These tags are used extensively for:
- Collision filtering.
- Gameplay logic decisions.
- Runtime behavior checks.
- Used while debugging

Tags include:
- TERRAIN_TAG
- TRUNK_TAG
- LEAF_TAG
- FRUIT_TAG
- AVATAR_TAG
- SKY_TAG
- SUN_TAG
- SUN_HALO_TAG
- NIGHT_TAG

This class is used by:
- Avatar (to distinguish terrain, trunks, and fruits during collisions).
- Fruit (to restrict collisions to the avatar).
- Day/night objects (sun, halo, night overlay).
- PepseGameManager (implicitly, via collision layer rules).

Main goal was to not destroy polymorphism.
I used tags as a part of the game engine instead of instanceof checks as we studied to avoid
so i needed to find a way sync between the different objects in the game so atlest they could
know what is the name of the thing i just collide in? 
in that way the it preserves polymorphism, while using game engine functionalities.

Why a centralized tag class:
- Preserving polymorphism
- Avoids duplicated string literals across the codebase.
- Creates a single source of truth for semantic object roles.

This design allows the engine’s collision system to be used idiomatically,
while keeping gameplay logic clean and decoupled.

------------------------------------------------
3.3 PepseGameConstants – global configuration and orchestration
------------------------------------------------

PepseGameConstants defines global configuration values that describe how the game
is assembled rather than how individual objects behave.

Its responsibilities include:
- Layer indices for all game object categories.
- Frame rate configuration.
- Day/night cycle length.
- Default avatar spawn location.

This class is primarily used by:
- PepseGameManager (world construction, layer configuration, camera setup).
- Flora (to avoid placing a trunk at the avatar spawn position).
- Display setup (energy display layer).

Examples of values centralized here:
- Layer ordering for sky, terrain, avatar, leaves, fruits, dispaly, sun, night overlay.
- Collision rules that depend on consistent layer assignment.
- Default avatar spawn X coordinate used consistently across systems.

Why this class exists:
Without PepseGameConstants, these values would either:
- Be duplicated across multiple classes, or
- Be hard-coded inside logic-heavy classes like PepseGameManager.

Both options would harm maintainability and clarity.

By extracting these values:
- The game manager becomes declarative rather than cluttered with magic numbers.
- Global changes for example:  layer ordering or cycle length, are easy and safe.
- Gameplay logic classes remain focused on behavior, not configuration.

------------------------------------------------
3.4 Design rationale – coordination without breaking encapsulation
------------------------------------------------

All three classes (WorldConstants, WorldTags, PepseGameConstants) share a common goal:

To allow independent systems to coordinate through shared agreements,
rather than through direct knowledge of each other.

They:
- Do not hold mutable state.
- Do not depend on gameplay classes.
- Do not contain logic that belongs to a specific entity.

Instead, they define:
- Common vocabulary (tags).
- Common geometry rules (world constants).
- Common configuration (game constants).

This approach preserves encapsulation while still allowing:
- Deterministic world generation.
- Stable infinite-world streaming.
- Clear separation between gameplay, world planning, and rendering concerns.

================================================
4. API changes and notable additions
================================================

4.1 ColorSupplier overloads for deterministic randomness
------------------------------------------------

To support deterministic randomness in an infinite, streamed world,
i extended the provided ColorSupplier class without changing its original behavior.

The original class relies on an internal static Random instance, which is unsuitable
for reproducible world generation when objects are created, destroyed, and recreated.

To address this, i added overloads that accept a caller-provided Random instance:
- public static Color approximateColor(Color baseColor, Random rnd)
- public static Color approximateColor(Color baseColor, int colorDelta, Random rnd)
- private static int randomChannelInRange(int min, int max, Random rnd)

These overloads reuse the original logic exactly:
- Same channel ranges.
- Same clamping to [0,255].
- Same color distribution.

The only difference is the randomness source.

This design allows:
- Deterministic color sampling per object and position.
- Stable visual appearance across world reloads.
- Full backward compatibility with existing code.

Similar to the way i handled the avatar energy flicker (Section 1.4.1),
the decision to modify ColorSupplier was made based on the official course Q&A forum.

During development, i needed deterministic randomness for world generation
(colors must be reproducible even when objects are unloaded and loaded again).
However, the provided ColorSupplier implementation uses an internal static Random,
which makes the produced colors dependent on creation order and runtime timing.

In the course Q&A forum, it was explicitly answered that one of the acceptable
solutions is to modify the provided ColorSupplier class, as long as
the change is documented in the README.

Therefore, i implemented the change as pure method overloading:
- The existing API behavior remains unchanged for all existing callers.
- New overloads allow callers to pass a Random instance explicitly.

This approach provides deterministic color sampling without introducing coupling
between unrelated systems, and without changing the distribution logic.

